= 13
{parent=Section A}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=13}

Using mass $7\,\mathrm u$ for the dominant <lithium> isotope gives a transition <wavenumber> $\widetilde\nu_0=14925\ \mathrm{cm^{-1}}$. Inserting the thermal speed into the stated <Doppler broadening> expression gives
$$
\Delta\widetilde\nu_D={\widetilde\nu_0\over c}\sqrt{\frac{2k_BT\ln2}{m}}
\simeq0.128\ \mathrm{cm^{-1}}.
$$
The supplied formula is the Gaussian <half width at half maximum>; the corresponding full width is about $0.256\ \mathrm{cm^{-1}}$. Meanwhile exponential population decay with lifetime $\tau$ produces a <natural linewidth> expressed as a full width
$$
\delta\widetilde\nu_{\rm nat}=\frac{1}{2\pi c\tau}\simeq1.97\times10^{-4}\ \mathrm{cm^{-1}}.
$$
Here $c$ must be in $\mathrm{cm\,s^{-1}}$ in the second expression. Equivalently the fine-structure separation is about $9.0$ GHz, the Doppler half width about $3.83$ GHz, and the <natural linewidth> expressed as a full width about $5.89$ MHz. The <natural linewidth> is therefore negligible compared with thermal broadening; low density also makes collision broadening less important.

**The doublet is resolvable in principle, but only marginally against the Doppler width.** The separation exceeds one Doppler full width, so two components can survive, although their profiles overlap strongly. The $J=3/2$ and $J=1/2$ upper levels have different <statistical weights>, so the weaker member need not make an equally conspicuous maximum. A <spectral resolving power> $R\gtrsim\widetilde\nu_0/0.3\simeq5\times10^4$ is the basic instrumental requirement; a width well below $0.1\ \mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ and adequate signal would make the test more convincing. An arbitrarily fine spectrometer cannot remove the physical Doppler overlap. This conclusion uses the stipulated thermal, optically thin picture; additional photospheric velocity fields or optical-depth effects would require more information.

= 14
{parent=Section A}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=14}

Use the <D3h molecular point group> for <phosphorus pentafluoride>, with $z$ along the two axial P–F bonds. For each <symmetry> operation, only atoms left in place contribute to the character of the Cartesian displacement representation; each contributes the trace of the corresponding $3\times3$ spatial <matrix>. In the class order $E,2C_3,3C_2',\sigma_h,2S_3,3\sigma_v$ this gives
$$
\chi_{3N}=(18,0,-2,4,-2,4).
$$
For example a $C_2'$ fixes P and one equatorial F, and each fixed atom has Cartesian trace $-1$, giving $-2$. <reflection> in the equatorial plane fixes four atoms, each with trace $1$, giving $4$. Reduction with the <character table> gives
$$
\Gamma_{3N}=2A_1'+A_2'+4E'+3A_2''+2E''.
$$
Subtract translations $E'+A_2''$ and rotations $A_2'+E''$ to obtain
$$
\boxed{\Gamma_{\rm vib}=2A_1'+3E'+2A_2''+E''.}
$$
Its dimension is $2+6+2+2=12=3(6)-6$, as required for a nonlinear six-atom molecule. Thus there are eight symmetry-distinct fundamental <frequencies> in the harmonic picture, with each E mode doubly degenerate. This analysis concerns vibrations about one trigonal-bipyramidal minimum; it does not treat large-amplitude rearrangements between minima.

= 15
{parent=Section A}
