= i
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

**<configuration interaction with singles and doubles> (<CISD>)** uses a linear combination of the reference <Slater determinant> and singly and doubly excited <determinants>. Its many-electron trial state is therefore not restricted to one <determinant>; the orbitals may be held fixed while the configuration coefficients are varied.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

**<Kohn-Sham equations> in <density functional theory>** use an auxiliary one-electron <Hamiltonian> whose effective potential depends on the density. Its orbitals construct a density representing an interacting electronic system; this is distinct from explicitly diagonalizing a large many-electron <determinant> <Hamiltonian>.<Self-Consistent Equations Including Exchange and Correlation Effects by Walter Kohn and Lu Jeu Sham>[1965, pp. A1133–A1135, ground-state effective one-electron formulation]{ref}

= iii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

**<molecular mechanics>** evaluates a parametrized potential of atomic positions, with terms such as bond stretching, angle bending, torsion and nonbonded interactions. <electrons> are represented implicitly through the fitted force field, rather than by electronic orbitals, a density or an electronic <wavefunction>. This example is different from both preceding electronic methods.

= b
{parent=19}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

For conventional <CISD>, the leading matrix-vector contraction scales as $O(o^2v^4)$ in occupied and virtual orbital dimensions. If both grow proportionally to a system-size parameter $N$, the familiar estimate is **$O(N^6)$ work per iteration**; the iteration count and integral-storage strategy add implementation-dependent costs.

Calculated <potential energy surfaces> can predict equilibrium geometries and harmonic force constants, permitting comparison with diffraction-derived structures, <rotational constants> and vibrational spectra. Calculating several roots or different charge states can also estimate excitation or <ionization energies>. Such predictions depend on basis and truncation quality; truncated <CI> is not generally size extensive and is not automatically reliable for strongly multiconfigurational bond breaking. The quoted polynomial scaling is for <CISD>, not full <configuration interaction>, whose <determinant> space grows combinatorially.

= 20
{parent=Section A}
{scope}

= a
{parent=20}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

An **<alternant hydrocarbon>** has a pi-site graph that can be divided into two sets so that every retained neighbouring resonance interaction joins different sets. Equivalently, the graph is bipartite. A chain and an even ring satisfy this condition; an odd ring does not. The classification refers to the model's coupling graph, rather than simply to which bonds are drawn double in one <Lewis structure>.

= b
{parent=20}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Write $H=\alpha I+K$ with the same <carbon> diagonal parameter throughout, and define $P$ to have entries $+1$ on one sublattice and $-1$ on the other. Bipartite connectivity implies
$$
PKP=-K.
$$
Thus $Kc=\epsilon c$ implies $K(Pc)=-\epsilon(Pc)$. This proves **energy pairing about $\alpha$**, $E=\alpha\pm\epsilon$, and **equal site-probability magnitudes in each paired pair**, since $|(Pc)_i|^2=|c_i|^2$. This is the Coulson-Rushbrooke pairing property.<Note on the method of molecular orbitals by Charles Alfred Coulson and G. S. Rushbrooke>[1940, pp. 193–200, alternant-system pairing and density properties]{ref}

For the usual neutral half-filled closed-shell occupation, the lower member of each pair is doubly occupied. Combining paired coefficients with completeness of the <orthonormal basis> gives **one pi <electron> per <carbon>**, so the model predicts no net pi charge separation. The same argument makes **off-diagonal <pi bond> orders between sites on the same sublattice zero**. These statements assume a neutral half-filled density and do not apply unchanged to the charged six-electron four-site ion considered separately.

An imbalance of sublattice sizes also guarantees **at least $|N_A-N_B|$ nonbonding orbitals at energy $\alpha$**, because the rectangular coupling block has insufficient <rank> to remove all its <null space>. None of the spectral pairing, equal paired densities, neutral charge uniformity or sublattice bond-order restrictions is required for a non-alternant graph. Long-range same-sublattice couplings or unequal diagonal parameters can likewise destroy the simple proof.

= Section B
{parent=Paper 2A}
{scope}

= 21
{parent=Section B}
{scope}

= a
{parent=21}
{scope}

= i
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

The coarse spacing is about $2\widetilde B$, so $\widetilde B\simeq0.25\ \mathrm{cm^{-1}}$. The two groups near 1.5 and $2.0\ \mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ therefore have **$J=2\to3$ and $J=3\to4$**, respectively. For positive $\widetilde D_{JK}$, increasing $K^2$ moves a component to lower <wavenumber>. The assignments are
$$
\boxed{\begin{array}{c|c|c}
J\to J+1&K&\widetilde\nu\ /\mathrm{cm^{-1}}\\ \hline
2\to3&0&1.50130\\
&1&1.50128\\
&2&1.50122\\ \hline
3\to4&0&2.00171\\
&1&2.00168\\
&2&2.00160\\
&3&2.00147
\end{array}}
$$
Within one group, successive separations scale as $(K+1)^2-K^2=1,3,5,\ldots$. The observed gaps follow that pattern to their printed precision. There are $J+1$ distinct $|K|$ components because $|K|\leq J$ in the lower state; the $+K$ and $-K$ members remain coincident without an applied field. A high-resolution feature here is therefore not simply assigned to an arbitrary magnetic sublevel.

\Image[paper-2a-rotational.png]
{title=Derived stick assignments of the two rotational groups, showing the K-squared pattern}

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The $K=0$ lines give two equations independent of $\widetilde D_{JK}$:
$$
1.50130=6\widetilde B-108\widetilde D_J,\qquad
2.00171=8\widetilde B-256\widetilde D_J.
$$
Solving yields **$\widetilde B\simeq0.25022\ \mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ and $\widetilde D_J\simeq2.1\times10^{-7}\ \mathrm{cm^{-1}}$**. The small distortion is obtained by subtracting close numbers, so its precision is considerably worse than that of $\widetilde B$.

For components in a fixed group,
$$
\widetilde\nu(J,0)-\widetilde\nu(J,K)=2\widetilde D_{JK}(J+1)K^2.
$$
For example the $J=2,K=2$ displacement gives $\widetilde D_{JK}=0.00008/24$, while $J=3,K=3$ gives $0.00024/72$. Both give **$\widetilde D_{JK}\simeq3.3\times10^{-6}\ \mathrm{cm^{-1}}$**; the smaller gaps are consistent after rounding. The pure rotational transitions have $\Delta K=0$, so the $(\widetilde A-\widetilde B)K^2$ and $\widetilde D_KK^4$ terms cancel. **Neither $\widetilde A$ nor $\widetilde D_K$ can be determined from these zero-field lines.**

= iii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

A spectral component shifts by the difference between its upper- and lower-level Stark shifts. With $\Delta J=+1$, $\Delta K=0$ and $\Delta M=q$, the supplied energy formula gives
$$
\delta\widetilde\nu=\frac{\mu E_{\rm field}}{hc}K\left[\frac{M+q}{(J+1)(J+2)}-\frac{M}{J(J+1)}\right].
$$
For radiation polarized parallel to the static field, $q=0$, so
$$
\boxed{\delta\widetilde\nu=-\frac{2\mu E_{\rm field}KM}{hc\,J(J+1)(J+2)}.}
$$
Thus **the assigned $K=0$ feature has no first-order splitting**, whereas the $K\ne0$ features acquire symmetrically arranged magnetic-sublevel components with spacings proportional to $|K|$. With perpendicular radiation, $q=\pm1$ gives the corresponding different allowed pattern. Recording field-dependent spectra with specified <polarization> would test both the J values and the K sequence; using an individual level's shift alone would not predict the observed line positions. Higher-order shifts of the nominally unsplit feature are outside this first-order test.

= iv
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

The largest applied field gives
$$
\frac{\mu E_{\rm field}}{hc}=\frac{(1.6)(3.33564\times10^{-30})(10^4)}{(6.62607015\times10^{-34})(2.99792458\times10^{10})}
\simeq2.69\times10^{-3}\ \mathrm{cm^{-1}}.
$$
For parallel <polarization>, adjacent allowed M values in a $J,K$ component are separated by
$$
\Delta_M\widetilde\nu=\frac{2(2.69\times10^{-3})|K|}{J(J+1)(J+2)}.
$$
Already at $K=1$ this is **$2.24\times10^{-4}\ \mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ for $J=2$** and **$8.96\times10^{-5}\ \mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ for $J=3$**. Both exceed the stated $2\times10^{-5}\ \mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ resolution, and larger $|K|$ gives larger first-order spacings. **The spectrometer should resolve suitable Stark components**, provided their physical linewidths and signal-to-noise permit it. This predicts resolvable splittings rather than incorrectly comparing the maximum level shift directly with instrumental resolution.

= b
{parent=21}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

A regularly repeated set of sharp features is characteristic of the unresolved **Q subbranches of a <perpendicular rovibrational band>**. The transition dipole is perpendicular to the <symmetry> axis, giving $\Delta K=\pm1$; for the <Q branch> $\Delta J=0$. Holding the <rotational constants> fixed between vibrational states cancels the $BJ(J+1)$ term, leaving
$$
\widetilde\nu_Q=\widetilde\nu_v+(\widetilde A-\widetilde B)\big[(K\pm1)^2-K^2\big]
=\widetilde\nu_v+(\widetilde A-\widetilde B)(\pm2K+1).
$$
Many J transitions therefore coincide within each K-changing Q subbranch in this approximation. The different K subbranches are separated by
$$
\boxed{\Delta\widetilde\nu_Q=2(\widetilde A-\widetilde B).}
$$
The P and <R branches> have $\Delta J=-1$ and $+1$, respectively. Their extra J-dependent terms spread many transitions across a broad background at modest resolution. A parallel band, with $\Delta K=0$, would not generate this repeated Q-subbranch spacing under the stated assumptions.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The subbranch spacing gives $\widetilde A-\widetilde B=4.9\ \mathrm{cm^{-1}}$, hence **$\widetilde A\simeq5.15\ \mathrm{cm^{-1}}$**. Rotation about the C–I <symmetry> axis has moment
$$
I_a=\frac{h}{8\pi^2c\widetilde A}\simeq5.44\times10^{-47}\ \mathrm{kg\,m^2}.
$$
C and I lie on this axis, so only the three <hydrogens> contribute to $I_a$. If $\theta$ is the I–C–H angle, their perpendicular distances are $r_{\rm CH}\sin\theta$. Taking integer <hydrogen> mass gives
$$
I_a=3m_Hr_{\rm CH}^2\sin^2\theta,\qquad
\sin^2\theta\simeq\frac{5.44\times10^{-47}}{3(1.66054\times10^{-27})(1.1\times10^{-10})^2}\simeq0.902.
$$
The molecular arrangement selects the obtuse solution, so **$\theta\simeq108^\circ$**. The supplementary acute angle describes the opposite axial direction, not the bond angle to iodine. This is a rigid-geometry estimate based on an effective <rotational constant> and the stipulated bond length, rather than a precision equilibrium-structure measurement.

= 22
{parent=Section B}
{scope}

= a
{parent=22}
{scope}

= i
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

In the Condon approximation the intensity from $v''=0$ to $v'$ contains the <Franck-Condon factor>
$$
|\langle\chi^A_{v'}|\chi^X_0\rangle|^2.
$$
The lower ground-state vibrational <wavefunction> is localized near its equilibrium bond length. If the two equilibrium lengths are similar, it overlaps strongly with the upper $v'=0$ <wavefunction>, but its overlap with highly excited upper vibrational states is usually small or suffers oscillatory cancellation. If the equilibrium lengths differ enough that a vertical excitation reaches the outer portion of the upper well, high-$v'$ transitions can be strong while the $0\to0$ overlap is small. Thus **the two ends of a long progression are not normally both intense**. This is an overlap argument, not a ban on either set of transitions; anharmonicity, a varying electronic transition moment and unusual <wavefunctions> can modify the intensity envelope.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Write the upper <Morse oscillator> term values in <wavenumber> units as
$$
G(v)=\widetilde\omega_e(v+\tfrac12)-a(v+\tfrac12)^2,\qquad a=\widetilde\omega_ex_e.
$$
The spacing is $G(v+1)-G(v)=\widetilde\omega_e-2a(v+1)$. A <Birge-Sponer plot> therefore falls linearly in this model and can be extrapolated to zero spacing. If its horizontal coordinate is $x=v+1/2$, the plotted line is $y=(\widetilde\omega_e-a)-2ax$. Its area from $x=0$ to its zero gives
$$
\boxed{\widetilde D_0=\frac{(\widetilde\omega_e-a)^2}{4a}.}
$$
Equivalently a plot against $v+1$ has intercept $\widetilde\omega_e$ and triangle area $\widetilde D_e=\widetilde\omega_e^2/(4a)$; subtracting $G(0)=\widetilde\omega_e/2-a/4$ recovers $\widetilde D_0$. These conventions distinguish dissociation from the potential minimum and from the lowest vibrational level.

Measured consecutive members fix a local slope and intercept, so unobserved members can be supplied by extrapolation under an explicit spacing law. The upper-state limit on the <absorption> scale is $\widetilde\nu_{\rm lim}=\widetilde\nu_{00}+\widetilde D_0$. A local high-v fit can likewise estimate the remaining energy above an observed member without knowing its absolute v index. Near dissociation a real potential need not remain Morse-like, so an extrapolated triangle has model uncertainty; missing lines must not be silently assumed consecutive.

= iii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Successive differences remove the unknown absolute origin and the unknown initial vibrational quantum number. The five spacings are $329.3$, $296.0$, $261.7$, $226.7$ and $190.7\ \mathrm{cm^{-1}}$. Plot them at relative mid-indices $x=n+1/2$. A straight-line fit gives
$$
\Delta\widetilde\nu(x)\simeq347.505-34.650x\quad\mathrm{cm^{-1}},\qquad x_*\simeq10.029.
$$
The curve reaches zero spacing at $x_*$. Since a quadratic Morse progression has a spacing equal to its derivative at the interval midpoint, integrating the fitted line from the first observed relative level $n=0$ to $x_*$ estimates the energy still needed to reach the continuous limit:
$$
\widetilde\nu_{\rm lim}\simeq56298.9+\int_0^{x_*}(347.505-34.650x)\,dx
=56298.9+\frac{347.505^2}{2(34.650)}
\simeq58041.5\ \mathrm{cm^{-1}}.
$$
Thus the appropriate graphical conclusion is **$\widetilde\nu_{\rm lim}\approx5.80\times10^4\ \mathrm{cm^{-1}}$**, not a many-digit threshold. Directly fitting a quadratic to the six measured transition positions puts its vertex at about $58042.8\ \mathrm{cm^{-1}}$, which checks the arithmetic but not the physical extrapolation law. The slight variation of second differences shows that extrapolating a constant anharmonicity beyond the observed range is approximate. No assignment of the arbitrary index n to an absolute upper-state v is needed.

\Image[paper-2a-dissociation.png]
{title=Original relative-index Birge–Sponer extrapolation and the inferred progression limit}

= b
{parent=22}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Let $T_{v',v''}=T_e+G'(v')-G''(v'')$. For a fixed row, moving one column to the right therefore gives
$$
T_{v',v''+1}-T_{v',v''}=-\big[G''(v''+1)-G''(v'')\big]
=-\widetilde\omega_e''+2\widetilde\omega_e''x_e''(v''+1).
$$
Thus **adjacent-column entries decrease by the lower-state vibrational spacing**, independent of the upper-state row in the ideal model. If the positive difference is instead defined as left minus right, it is $\widetilde\omega_e''-2\widetilde\omega_e''x_e''(v''+1)$. Stating the subtraction direction prevents an artificial negative vibrational <frequency>.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Averaging the left-minus-right differences across the three rows gives lower-state spacings of about $1613.0$ and $1591.7\ \mathrm{cm^{-1}}$. Their difference is $2\widetilde\omega_e''x_e''$, so
$$
\boxed{\widetilde\omega_e''x_e''\simeq10.7\ \mathrm{cm^{-1}},\qquad
\widetilde\omega_e''\simeq1634\ \mathrm{cm^{-1}}.}
$$
Similarly, averaging successive down-column differences across the three columns gives upper-state spacings of about $1750.3$ and $1710.7\ \mathrm{cm^{-1}}$. Hence
$$
\boxed{\widetilde\omega_e'x_e'\simeq19.8\ \mathrm{cm^{-1}},\qquad
\widetilde\omega_e'\simeq1790\ \mathrm{cm^{-1}}.}
$$
Using the first row and first column alone would give approximately $(1637,11)$ and $(1790,20.5)\ \mathrm{cm^{-1}}$, respectively. The small inconsistency between nominally identical spacings is already present in the entries; it should be treated as limited data/model precision rather than fitted by assigning different lower-state constants to different rows. Values near **$(1.63\times10^3,11)$ for the lower state and $(1.79\times10^3,20)$ for the upper state** express the supported precision.

= 23
{parent=Section B}
{scope}

= a
{parent=23}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Choose x along O–O. The valence orbital order appropriate to O$_2$ is approximately
$$
2\sigma_g(2s)<2\sigma_u^*(2s)<3\sigma_g(2p_x)<1\pi_u(2p_y,2p_z)<1\pi_g^*(2p_y,2p_z)<3\sigma_u^*(2p_x).
$$
Its twelve valence <electrons> occupy
$$
\boxed{(2\sigma_g)^2(2\sigma_u^*)^2(3\sigma_g)^2(1\pi_u)^4(1\pi_{g,y}^*)^1(1\pi_{g,z}^*)^1.}
$$
The two degenerate pi-antibonding orbitals are singly occupied with parallel spins according to <Hund's rules>. Thus **$S=1$, <multiplicity> $2S+1=3$**, with three possible $M_S$ values. A triplet is a many-electron spin classification, not three <electrons> in one orbital. The resulting ground term is ${}^3\Sigma_g^-$. Exchange stabilization favours this triplet over the singlets formed within the same frontier-orbital manifold.

\Image[paper-2a-orbitals.png]
{title=Original valence-O2 diagram with the two singly occupied pi-antibonding orbitals}

= b
{parent=23}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The active set contains four <oxygen> pi orbitals and two <carbon> pi orbitals, with **eight <electrons> in six orbitals**. The noninteracting levels, using the parameters inferred below, are
$$
\begin{array}{c|c|c}
\text{fragment orbitals}&E\ /\mathrm{eV}&\text{occupation}\\ \hline
\pi_{O,z},\ \pi_{O,y}&-16.1&2,2\\
\pi_{O,z}^*,\ \pi_{O,y}^*&-12.1&1,1\quad(\text{parallel spins})\\
\pi_C&-10.5&2\\
\pi_C^*&-5.5&0
\end{array}
$$
Each bonding <oxygen> orbital has equal-phase coefficients on its two O atoms; its antibonding partner reverses one coefficient. The two pi directions are z in the prospective ring plane and y perpendicular to it. <carbon> pi and pi-star use z-directed <p orbitals> with equal and opposite coefficient signs. In this separated-fragment comparison the occupations are those of neutral triplet O$_2$ plus neutral <ethene>. **Do not refill the combined list solely by energy**, which would move an <electron> between infinitely separated fragments and describe a charge-transfer state instead.

\Image[paper-2a-active-orbitals.png]
{title=Original signed-orbital sketches for the six reactant and six product active orbitals}

= c
{parent=23}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Use negative bonding <Hückel resonance integrals>, $\beta_{OO}=-2.0$ and $\beta_{CC}=-2.5\ \mathrm{eV}$. The <oxygen> HOMO is pi-antibonding, whereas the <ethene> HOMO is pi-bonding. The frozen-orbital Koopmans estimate therefore gives
$$
\alpha_O-\beta_{OO}\simeq-12.1,\qquad
\alpha_C+\beta_{CC}\simeq-10.5.
$$
Consequently **$\alpha_O\simeq-14.1\ \mathrm{eV}$ and $\alpha_C\simeq-8.0\ \mathrm{eV}$**. These <Hückel Coulomb parameters> are effective parameters of this spectroscopically calibrated model. Real <ionization energies> include relaxation and correlation, so they are not exact <matrix> elements of an isolated-atom <Hamiltonian>.

= d
{parent=23}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

Let $\sigma_L,\sigma_R$ be localized bonding orbitals of the two new C–O bonds, and let $\sigma_L^*,\sigma_R^*$ be their antibonding partners. Their <symmetry> combinations are
$$
\sigma_\pm=\frac{\sigma_L\pm\sigma_R}{\sqrt2},\qquad
\sigma_\pm^*=\frac{\sigma_L^*\pm\sigma_R^*}{\sqrt2}.
$$
The two remaining active orbitals are out-of-ring <oxygen> lone-pair combinations, $n_\pm=(p_{y,L}\pm p_{y,R})/\sqrt2$. The opposite-phase combination $n_-$ is the HOMO in the supplied ordering and $n_+$ is HOMO−1. Both are filled, as are the two C–O bonding combinations, giving
$$
\boxed{(\sigma_+)^2(\sigma_-)^2(n_+)^2(n_-)^2(\sigma_+^*)^0(\sigma_-^*)^0.}
$$
The original active-orbital sketches show these phase relations, while the correlation diagram gives a qualitative energy ordering. Precise splittings between unrelated <symmetry> blocks are not determined by the fragment <ionization energies>. The retained O–O <sigma bond>, the C–C <sigma bond> and the other framework orbitals lie outside this six-orbital active comparison; the eight-electron count does not describe all valence <electrons> of the product.

= e
{parent=23}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=e}

The symmetric approach preserves the <C2v molecular point group> with its twofold axis z and mirror planes xz and yz. The molecular ring lies in xz. Classify orbitals by <reflection> signs $(\sigma_{xz},\sigma_{yz})$: $A_1=(+,+)$, $B_1=(+,-)$, $B_2=(-,+)$, $A_2=(-,-)$. With globally defined p-orbital phases the <molecular orbital correlation diagram> gives
$$
\boxed{\begin{array}{c|c|c}
\text{reactant orbital}&C_{2v}&\text{product orbital}\\ \hline
\pi_{O,z}&A_1&\sigma_+\\
\pi_C&A_1&\sigma_+^*\\
\pi_{O,z}^*&B_1&\sigma_-\\
\pi_C^*&B_1&\sigma_-^*\\
\pi_{O,y}&B_2&n_+\\
\pi_{O,y}^*&A_2&n_-
\end{array}}
$$
Within either two-orbital <symmetry> block the lower member remains correlated with the lower product member; the <non-crossing rule> prevents swapping their order through an ordinary same-symmetry crossing. Crossings between different <irreducible representations> are allowed. The crucial consequence is that the occupied <carbon> pi orbital correlates to a C–O-antibonding combination, rather than both initially occupied $A_1$ orbitals becoming the two product bonding orbitals.

\Image[paper-2a-correlation.png]
{title=Original six-orbital correlation and schematic state correlation for the symmetric oxygen addition}

= f
{parent=23}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=f}

**Endpoint energies, endpoint phase patterns and a conserved <symmetry> fix the qualitative connectivity.** Each initial orbital must end in the appropriate product <symmetry> block, and the ordering within a generic block is preserved by avoided crossings. The two singleton blocks $A_2$ and $B_2$ have unique partners. No numerical calculation at each intermediate separation is required to draw those connections.

This conclusion is limited to a correlation diagram: it does not determine intermediate orbital energies, interaction strengths, the location or height of a <reaction energy barrier>, or the lowest route on a multidimensional <potential energy surface>. If the reaction breaks the assumed <symmetry>, previously distinct <symmetry> blocks can mix and the diagram must be reconsidered.

= g
{parent=23}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=g}

In the <electronic state correlation diagram>, the singly occupied reactant orbitals for triplet <oxygen> have <symmetries> $B_1$ and $A_2$, so their spatial product is $B_2$. The reactant state is therefore a triplet ${}^3B_2$, whereas the closed-shell product is ${}^1A_1$. **A strictly spin-conserving path cannot correlate triplet reactants directly to the ground-state product.** It correlates to an excited triplet product configuration or requires <intersystem crossing>; the symmetric direct reaction consequently has a substantial electronic obstacle.

Simply changing the <oxygen> spin does not repair the occupied carbon-orbital correlation. The lowest <oxygen> singlet manifold has spatial components of $A_1$ and $B_2$ <symmetry> in this subgroup. The $A_1$ singlet can have an avoided crossing with another $A_1$ configuration and reach the product singlet; the $B_2$ singlet cannot mix directly with the product ${}^1A_1$ while $C_{2v}$ is maintained. Following either closed-shell <oxygen> singlet component in the orbital diagram still leaves the carbon-pi <electron> pair in the rising $\sigma_+^*$ orbital. Its redistribution into the final occupied orbitals requires configuration mixing at an avoided crossing. **A barrier is therefore expected even for the symmetry-constrained concerted singlet addition; singlet does not imply barrierless.** The state curves in the original diagram are schematic, illustrating this avoided-crossing argument rather than computed energies.<Selection rules for singlet-oxygen reactions. Concerted addition reactions by David R. Kearns>[1969, orbital and state correlation for concerted olefin additions]{ref}

The real singlet reaction can lower this obstacle through asymmetric or stepwise pathways that are absent from the stipulated direct approach. A primary multireference study explicitly distinguishes singlet and triplet addition through biradical intermediates, supporting that limitation of a one-coordinate <symmetry> argument.<Singlet and triplet potential surfaces for the O2+C2H4 reaction by Kyoyeon Park and others>[2010, abstract and calculated addition/isomerization surfaces]{ref} Neither correlation alone nor a spin label supplies a numerical <activation energy>.

= 24
{parent=Section B}
{scope}

= a
{parent=24}
{scope}

= i
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

The missing kinetic contribution is **$\tfrac12m_b\dot y_b^2$**. Both atoms contribute independent translational kinetic energy before the coordinates are changed.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Substituting $y_i=q_i/\sqrt{m_i}$ gives
$$
\boxed{E=\frac12\dot q_a^2+\frac12\dot q_b^2+
\frac{k_1}{2}\left(\frac{q_b}{\sqrt{m_b}}-\frac{q_a}{\sqrt{m_a}}-\bar r\right)^2.}
$$
The shifted equilibrium coordinates satisfy $\bar q_b/\sqrt{m_b}-\bar q_a/\sqrt{m_a}=\bar r$. Expanding about them replaces q by displacements without changing the kinetic metric or the Hessian.

= iii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Taking the Hessian of the potential with respect to mass-weighted displacements gives
$$
\boxed{D_0=k_1\begin{pmatrix}1/m_a&-1/\sqrt{m_am_b}\\-1/\sqrt{m_am_b}&1/m_b\end{pmatrix}.}
$$
The equations of motion are $\ddot{\boldsymbol q}=-D_0\boldsymbol q$, so <eigenvalues> are squared <angular frequencies>. Normalized <eigenvectors> and <frequencies> are
$$
\boxed{u_T=\frac{(\sqrt{m_a},\sqrt{m_b})}{\sqrt{m_a+m_b}},\quad\omega_T=0;\qquad
u_S=\frac{(\sqrt{m_b},-\sqrt{m_a})}{\sqrt{m_a+m_b}},\quad
\omega_S=\sqrt{k_1\left(\frac1{m_a}+\frac1{m_b}\right)}=\sqrt{\frac{k_1}{\mu}}.}
$$
Both vectors are in the q basis, and $\mu=m_am_b/(m_a+m_b)$. The translation corresponds to equal physical displacements $\delta y_a=\delta y_b$. The stretch has $m_a\delta y_a+m_b\delta y_b=0$ and opposite physical displacements with magnitude ratio $|\delta y_a/\delta y_b|=m_b/m_a$. Ordinary <frequencies> are $\nu=\omega/(2\pi)$; <spectroscopic wavenumbers> are $\omega/(2\pi c)$.

\Image[paper-2a-normal-modes.png]
{title=Original normal mode displacements for free and weakly surface-bound diatomic models}

= b
{parent=24}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

With equal masses m, the two mass-weighted Hessians are
$$
\boxed{D_{(1)}=\frac1m\begin{pmatrix}k_1+k_2&-k_1\\-k_1&k_1\end{pmatrix},\qquad
D_{(2)}=\frac1m\begin{pmatrix}k_1+k_3&-k_1\\-k_1&k_1+k_3\end{pmatrix}.}
$$
For a uniform displacement vector $t=(1,1)$,
$$
D_{(1)}t=\frac1m(k_2,0),\qquad D_{(2)}t=\frac{k_3}{m}(1,1).
$$
Therefore **uniform translation is not a <normal mode> in orientation (1)** when $k_2\ne0$: anchoring only one atom mixes translation with internal stretching. **It is an exact <normal mode> displacement in orientation (2)** because both atoms have identical restoring terms. Its <frequency> is nevertheless nonzero, $\omega_T=\sqrt{k_3/m}$; the antisymmetric stretch has $\omega_S=\sqrt{(2k_1+k_3)/m}$. The rigid surface breaks free translational invariance in both orientations. A vector resembling a uniform translation need not be a zero-frequency mode when the molecule is anchored.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

For weak anchoring, $k_2\ll k_1$, use the normalized free-molecule <eigenvectors> $u_T=(1,1)/\sqrt2$ and $u_S=(1,-1)/\sqrt2$. The perturbation is
$$
\delta D=\frac{k_2}{m}\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\0&0\end{pmatrix}.
$$
Its diagonal <matrix> elements in this basis are both $k_2/(2m)$. First-order <time-independent perturbation theory> for the <eigenvalues> therefore gives
$$
\boxed{\omega_T^2\simeq\frac{k_2}{2m},\qquad
\omega_S^2\simeq\frac{2k_1}{m}+\frac{k_2}{2m}.}
$$
Hence **$\omega_T\simeq\sqrt{k_2/(2m)}$** and **$\omega_S\simeq\sqrt{2k_1/m}\,[1+k_2/(8k_1)]$**. The zero free translation requires taking a square root of its new <eigenvalue>, rather than Taylor-expanding the <frequency> about zero. It is not a degenerate <eigenvalue> in the stipulated one-dimensional two-coordinate model.

As a check, the exact squared <frequencies> are
$$
\omega_\pm^2=\frac{2k_1+k_2\pm\sqrt{4k_1^2+k_2^2}}{2m}.
$$
Expansion gives the same first-order results; the next lower-mode correction is $-k_2^2/(8k_1m)$. The perturbed <eigenvectors> contain small translation/stretch admixtures of order $k_2/(4k_1)$, consistent with the failure of uniform translation to remain an exact mode in orientation (1).
